## 3 Mistakes 1.5 Carat Asscher Cut Diamond Ring Buyers Always Make

These are the 3 price mistakes that send buyers to the wrong stone, the wrong budget, or the wrong clarity grade. Avoid all three before you look at a single diamond.

### Mistake 1: Expecting Natural 1.5ct at 1ct Prices

This is the single biggest shock in the asscher category. Buyers research 1ct asschers, see stones starting around $2,500, then search for 1.5ct naturals expecting a modest step up.

The reality: natural 1.5ct asscher cut diamonds with VS1 clarity and G color start at $12,000–18,000 for the stone alone. The price-per-carat jumps sharply at the 1.5ct threshold because natural stones of this size are significantly rarer.

There is a clean solution — and it is The Lab Advantage. The [IGI 1.52ct D-IF at $2,795](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) gives you 52% more carat weight than a natural 1ct asscher at a lower price than most natural 1ct VS1 stones.

This is not a compromise. It is a better stone by every measurable grade at a fraction of the natural 1.5ct cost.

The buyer who avoids Mistake 1 shops lab first at the 1.5ct size and treats natural as the premium tier — not the default.

### Mistake 2: Going Below VS1 at 1.5ct

The Clarity Floor that applies at 1ct — VS1 minimum — applies at 1.5ct with even less room for error.

At 1ct, a centered VS2 inclusion might be borderline eye-clean depending on position. At 1.5ct, the step facets are larger, the reflections travel further, and any inclusion near the table is more visible — not less. The hall of mirrors effect that makes the asscher beautiful at 1ct becomes more pronounced at 1.5ct.

[![Asscher cut diamond ring in yellow gold plain solitaire setting worn on model's finger](/images/asscher-cut-diamond-ring-yellow-gold-solitaire-on-finger.jpg)](https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/asscher-cut-diamond-rings?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational)

VS1 is the floor. VVS2 is ideal at 1.5ct. This is exactly why The Lab Advantage matters: the [IGI 1.52ct D-IF](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) starts at the highest clarity grade possible.

There is no clarity risk to inspect or manage.

If you are buying natural at the 1.5ct size, inspect the stone's specific video and GIA certificate inclusion plot before purchasing. Never order a natural 1.5ct asscher with VS2 or below without a face-up video review.

### Mistake 3: Choosing H Color Without Checking Your Metal

The color floor for asscher cuts is G in white metal. This is true at 1ct and it is more true at 1.5ct.

At 1ct, the G floor is a guideline. At 1.5ct, it becomes a firm rule. The larger face-up table gives the step facets more surface area to reflect any tint.

H color that might pass in a 1ct asscher in white gold becomes visibly warm at 1.5ct in the same setting.

In yellow gold, you can sometimes go to H — the warm metal absorbs the stone's tint and the combination looks intentional. In white gold or platinum, stay at G or better. The [IGI 1.52ct D-IF](https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/27991043?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational) eliminates this concern entirely — D color at 1.5ct in any metal is clean by definition.

