/td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/tapered-cathedral-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-item-203288?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">Cathedral Solitaire YG</a></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/engagement-rings/design-your-own-ring/tapered-cathedral-solitaire-engagement-ring-in-14k-yellow-gold-item-203288?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$1,240</a></td><td><strong>$4,865</strong></td><td>Cathedral YG + 1.84ct — formal, elevated, best YG ring in table</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** The lab 1.5ct asscher ring starts at $3,655 with the entry configuration. The Art Deco setting at $3,985 is the complete ring this shape was designed for. Stepping to 1.84ct adds $830 to the stone price and takes you to $4,815 with the Art Deco setting — still under $5,000 for a stone 21% larger than the 1.52ct entry.

> "I tell lab asscher buyers the same thing every time: the 1.52ct D-IF is the correct answer at 1.5ct. You are not compromising on clarity or color. You are getting a better-graded stone than 99% of natural asschers buyers could afford at this size. The step facets will show you exactly how clean that Internally Flawless grade is. It is not a subtle difference — it is visible." — Farzana Hasan, **[Diamond Critics](https://diamondcritics.com/about-farzana/)**

